in india a huge second wave of covet 19  swept through the country thousands died  crematoriums to burn the dead were  overwhelmed by the numbers they held  mass cremations sometimes people had to  make do and burn their relatives bodies  where they could ceremonies were rushed  and sometimes makeshift they continued  into the night  one crematorium worker tells dw's  namisha jaiswal his story  rajesh ojala has done with his hospital  shift for the day  working in patient care is demanding  but manageable he says  it is a far cry from the 18 months he  spent working in the mortuary through  the worst of the pandemic here  ujjala has only worked two years in  healthcare  in another life he was a performance  artist  ojala says he took to the stage at the  age of 10 and loved every bit of it  as an organizer makeup artist and dancer  all of this came to a standstill when  the pandemic hit  and ojala needed work  when a friend refused to work in the  mortuary of delhi's biggest coronavirus  hospital  ujala stepped in  during the second wave  he recounts losing track of the number  of bodies coming in  piled into  ambulances ojala worked frantically to  provide dignity to the victims families  in the chaos  there was no end to his shifts  in a space for a hundred bodies there  were two or three hundred  piled one on top of the other  and my job was to sort through these  identify and mark them  that  was the worst day of my life  and then it got  worse ujjala's younger brother took ill  ojala pleaded for a bed at the  overwhelmed hospital he worked at  but it was too late  his brother fought for life for five  days  days in which ojala wasn't given the  time off to visit him  endlessly when he started working in the  crematorium  losing her youngest compounded her fears  i never imagined this would happen to me  the second coronavirus wave will haunt  me till the day i die  i'm still terrified i have only two  children left i just want them to be  safe  i just want to spend my last years with  them  experts say that the second wave however  may be a key reason why cases are  currently low in india  tests show that coronavirus antibodies  have been found in large sections of the  indian population  essentially acting like an additional  vaccine dose  this could blunt the rise in cases  expected with omicron here  hybrid immunity which essentially means  a natural infection followed by  vaccination provides as good a  protection as three shots of vaccines  and that is a reason enough to believe  that  indian population is right now protected  in the future it might require vaccine  booster ojala is already seeing omicron  cases rise in his hospital  and doesn't expect to return to the  stage anytime soon  his hopes for 2022 are far simpler  he suspects that even this though may be  too ambitious  some extraordinary images there for more  let's bring in dw correspondent namisha  jaiswal in new delhi uh namisha as we  were just reminded in your report this  was a very tough year for india and  though the silver lining may be that  there is much hybrid immunity  authorities can't ignore the that  omicron cases are on the rise  what's the government doing about this  well michael the indian government has  responded fast to when the omicron  variant was first announced very quickly  new restrictions and guidelines were  imposed on international travel  including stricter norms for both  testing and isolation now with cases  nearing 600 in omicron alone the indian  government has also announced that  different states are going to impose  night curfews depending on the case load  that they are facing themselves now some  states have also announced restrictions  on the capacities of spas malls  restaurants new year and christmas  celebrations are also likely to be  impacted especially in big cities like  delhi and mumbai  you know i'm curious how are indians  themselves currently responding to the  omicron threat especially after this  devastating covet-19 year for them  well the second wave was definitely  devastating michael but over the last  six months india has seen a lower case  load and this has eased off  inappropriate behavior and has also  allowed for people to come out more  restrictions have been almost completely  lifted before these new recent  restrictions related to omicron we saw  massive crowds at markets and  restaurants and shopping areas  during india's festive season in  november and it continued into christmas  and the new year as well people are of  course very apprehensive about new  restrictions and lockdowns especially as  it impacted business immensely but of  course even if the people are not ready  to take up over appropriate behavior yet  the government's moves will be a  different story  after having to deal with up to half a  million deaths by the coronavirus so far  what's the progress on vaccinations  india's progress on the vaccination  front has had its peaks and troughs but  overall 60 percent of the eligible  population which is all adults have been  completely vaccinated 90 people have at  least gotten one dose of the adults that  have that are eligible or in a christmas  address the prime minister also  announced that starting on the third of  january  children from the ages of 15 to 18 will  also start getting vaccinated boosters  have also been announced for health care  workers as well as for those above 60  who are vulnerable and the scope of  these will continue to be increased  however india has only got the viral  vector vaccinations and no mrna  vaccinations that are considered to be  better for a third dose more on this  from experts in the government of course  as the new year progresses  many many thanks that is uh dw  correspondent namisha jaiswell in new  delhi  you
 

